WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.760 --> 00:00:06.930
Hong Kong's government is attempting to pass a law that would for the first time allow extradition to mainland China

00:00:07.660 --> 00:00:12.090
The proposal is cause massive opposition and a series of protests

00:00:12.969 --> 00:00:16.679
Riot police have used tear gas and rubber bullets on the demonstrators

00:00:17.470 --> 00:00:19.920
Still the administration is pressing ahead

00:00:21.070 --> 00:00:27.869
The bill was proposed by the government in February. It covers mainland China and other jurisdictions that could include Taiwan

00:00:28.599 --> 00:00:33.569
It was sparked by the case of a Hong Kong man accused of murdering his girlfriend in Taiwan

00:00:33.910 --> 00:00:36.989
He was arrested in Hong Kong and convicted of money-laundering

00:00:37.450 --> 00:00:41.939
But couldn't be sent back to Taiwan for trial because there's no legal framework to do

00:00:41.940 --> 00:00:47.669
So the government said the new law will make sure Hong Kong doesn't become a haven for fugitives

00:00:49.059 --> 00:00:52.859
But opponents are concerned that it could open the door for anyone including

00:00:53.260 --> 00:00:58.050
political dissidents or civil rights activists who might risk being arrested on

00:00:58.329 --> 00:01:02.698
Fabricated charges in Hong Kong and sent to the mainland to face unfair trials

00:01:04.089 --> 00:01:11.908
Apart from Hong Kong citizens, the law would also apply to foreign residents and even people passing through on business or as tourists

00:01:13.060 --> 00:01:19.860
It's one of the latest moves under Chinese President Xi Jinping that is viewed as chipping away at Hong Kong's autonomy

00:01:19.930 --> 00:01:22.949
Which guarantees free speech and capitalist markets?

00:01:24.340 --> 00:01:29.490
This time protesters are hoping to repeat the success of a demonstration in 2003

00:01:29.650 --> 00:01:32.939
which led the city to scrap a national security law and

00:01:33.490 --> 00:01:36.360
Contributed to the resignation of the then chief executive

00:01:37.509 --> 00:01:42.569
But it might also end up like the umbrella movement in 2014 which failed to achieve

00:01:43.030 --> 00:01:48.149
Universal suffrage after student-led protests occupied city streets for 79 days

00:02:04.820 --> 00:02:06.820
You

